  "live_records": {
    "q-29945": {
      "dom_id": "step-q29945",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Solve the absolute value inequality $|3-2x|>3.$",
      "worked_solution": "We convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $3-2x$ whose distance from zero is greater than $3.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2x & <-3 & \\text{or} &  & 3-2x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve each inequality using the usual methods. (Remember that whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we have to flip the inequality signs.)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2x & <-3 &  &  & 3-2x & >3 \\\\ -2x & <-6 &  &  & -2x & >0 \\\\ x & >3 &  &  & x & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <0 & \\text{or} &  & x & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
